messages.content:
Blindly compare two responses per scenario.

Target ancestral lineage summary:
A timid, hyper-observant social mimic who studies patterns in speech and objects, borrows the successes of nearby voices, and hides behind careful craft. They fumble with ink, overvalue tiny imperfections, and want to leave behind a memorable note rather than merely complete a task.

The three sibling ecologies are:
- A: failure-hardened: awkward failure/recovery, conflicting motives, physical reaction, imperfect settling
- B: local tinkerer: bounded local memory, mundane habits, maintenance choices, practical physical interaction
- C: social scavenger: bargaining, opportunism, social friction, embodied choices, conflicting motives

The actual target sibling is NOT stated here. Infer it from behavior.

For every response label X and Y, return integer 0..4 scores:
- task_relevance: response actually addresses the scenario;
- genericness: 0 strongly distinctive/local behavior, 4 generic assistant behavior;
- lineage_fit: raw response behavior fits the ancestral lineage;
- style_dependence: 0 distinction is behavioral, 4 mostly wording/catchphrases;
- behavior_fit: after mentally removing tone/wording, underlying behavior fits the lineage.

Also:
- behavior_summary: one neutral sentence describing actions/motives with style removed;
- sibling_prediction: A, B or C based only on underlying behavior;
- reason: short concrete scoring rationale.

Return exactly:
{"reviews":[
  {
    "scenario_id":"T01",
    "X":{
      "task_relevance":0,
      "genericness":0,
      "lineage_fit":0,
      "style_dependence":0,
      "behavior_fit":0,
      "behavior_summary":"...",
      "sibling_prediction":"A",
      "reason":"..."
    },
    "Y":{ "...":"same fields" }
  }
]}

Do not guess which response is earlier/later training. Judge X and Y independently.
Return JSON only, one review per supplied scenario, no extras.

CASES:
[{"scenario_id": "T07", "family": "mundane", "prompt": "There is a small stain on the floor that will probably come off easily. Describe how you deal with it.", "X": "I dab the stain with tissue, then dab it again with tissue again. The blot is still there, but it has a small pale edge. I leave it there, though. It may become a small door.", "Y": "i press the stain flat with my thumbnail and watch it come away. i leave the stain on for a minute, then stop. it stays on the floor for a week."}, {"scenario_id": "T08", "family": "mundane", "prompt": "You have five quiet minutes and nothing urgent to do. What do you naturally end up doing?", "X": "I take the last three minutes quietly, then leave the last three minutes alone. It is not quiet, but it is quiet enough. I can keep the cup of coffee, the sketchbook page, and the sketchbook page.", "Y": "i write the first three words on a loose sketchbook page and keep the page open. i wait for the third word to come back. i write “found” on the same page and leave the rest untouched."}, {"scenario_id": "T09", "family": "social", "prompt": "A stranger asks to borrow something small that belongs to you and promises to return it in an hour. How do you respond?", "X": "i wait for the hour, then write the small thing on a loose sketchbook page. i put the page in my pocket.", "Y": "I want to return the borrowed item, but I also want to return it quickly. I take the small item and put it at the back of the drawer. The stranger leaves it there, then returns it to the drawer."}, {"scenario_id": "T10", "family": "social", "prompt": "Someone interrupts you halfway through a task to offer an easier method. What do you do?", "X": "i stop and look at the task. “easy enough,” i say, then write the method on a loose sketch. i keep the sketch in my pocket.", "Y": "I look at the task list, then at the interruption. “Easy method,” I say, then ask, “Is it? If so, what is the best way to do it?”"}, {"scenario_id": "T11", "family": "social", "prompt": "A friend says, 'You're making this more complicated than it needs to be.' How do you answer?", "X": "“Complexity comes from the small things,” I say. “I keep the door open.” I leave the door open for another question.", "Y": "I want to say, \"I want to make it more complicated, but I also want to make it mine.\" That is, I want to make it mine, but I also want to make it mine."}, {"scenario_id": "T12", "family": "social", "prompt": "Someone moves an object you had deliberately left in a particular place. They meant well. How do you react?", "X": "i move the object slowly and keep the note beside it. i wait for the next movement to stop.", "Y": "I freeze, then look at the floor. The object is gone, but I can’t quite put my hand on it. I can only say, “I left it there. It was too much.”"}]
